---
export const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Is there a free trial?",
    answer:
      "No free trial. You can download and install the app to look around, but you need a license to actually use it. There's a 14-day money-back guarantee, so if it's not for you, email support@picmal.app for a full refund.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need to buy a license to use Picmal?",
    answer:
      "Yes. The download is free, but you need a license key to activate it. Once you buy, it's yours — no recurring fees, no expiration.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is Picmal available for Windows or Linux?",
    answer:
      "Mac only. No plans to change that.",
  },
  {
    question: "What formats does Picmal support?",
    answer:
      "Over 100. On the image side: JPEG, PNG, WebP, AVIF, HEIC, PSD, RAW, SVG, PDF, and more. Video: MP4, MOV, AVI, MKV, WebM, and others. Audio: MP3, WAV, AAC, FLAC, OGG, OPUS, plus a bunch more.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I resize images in Picmal?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Five modes: by percentage, fixed width, fixed height, max dimensions, or exact dimensions. You can resize, convert, and compress in one pass.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I compress videos and audio files?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Images compress in JPEG, PNG, WebP, GIF, SVG, TIFF, HEIC, AVIF, and PDF. Videos use H.264, audio uses AAC. You control the quality slider so you decide the tradeoff between size and fidelity.",
  },
  {
    question: "Where are my files processed?",
    answer:
      "On your Mac. Nothing gets uploaded. Your files never leave your machine.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I convert multiple files at once?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Drag in a pile of files or whole folders and convert them all at once. Each file shows its own progress so you can see what's happening.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I compress without converting?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Pick compression, adjust the quality slider, and the file stays in its original format — just smaller.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I remove metadata from my files?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Picmal strips metadata from images — GPS coordinates, camera info, timestamps, all of it. Smaller files, less data floating around.",
  },
  {
    question: "What macOS versions are supported?",
    answer:
      "macOS Sonoma (14.0) or later. Runs natively on Apple Silicon and works fine on Intel Macs too.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need an internet connection to use Picmal?",
    answer:
      "Not after you install it. Picmal runs entirely offline.",
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: faq.question,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      "@type": "Answer",
      text: faq.answer,
    },
  })),
};
---

<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
